// endpoint_report_page_state.proto — ReportPageState: Emacs tells the daemon
// whether it holds a workspace's page in its web view, and why. HOST section.
//
// WHY A PAGE IS LOADED IS AN EDITOR FACT. Emacs decides every page load and
// unload itself (page-unload.el: a page loads when the user shows its
// workspace, when its session comes up for work, or when its slot is made
// while the page is wanted, and unloads when its workspace goes dormant off
// screen or stops being visible), so a workspace's visibility makes its page
// ELIGIBLE and never says whether the page is held. This report is how the
// daemon's metrics (agentrepl_pages_loaded, agentrepl_page_loads_total,
// agentrepl_page_unloads_total) and the sidebar's count of loaded pages learn
// why each page was loaded and what Emacs asked the web view to hold.
//
// THE WEB HOST'S FRAMES ARE THE TRUTH OF WHICH PAGES ARE HELD. Emacs records a
// page held before the host has it (a call made while no host stands waits for
// the host's replay, and a host reload retires every frame until it), and a
// host call that failed leaves the host short of what Emacs asked. So the
// counts read the frames the web host's census names (webapp/src/host/
// load-census.ts, `webhost.census` `frames`), each labelled with the reason
// this report gave, and the daemon records any disagreement between the two
// that stands (daemon/internal/pageheld).
//
// THE STATE IS SCOPED TO THE WORKSPACE'S HOST STREAM. The daemon holds what
// this report says only while a WatchHostWorkspace stream of the workspace
// stands, and forgets it when the last one ends, so an editor that exits or a
// daemon that hands its workspaces on can never leave a page counted that no
// web view holds. So Emacs RESTATES what it holds (the `held` or `not_held`
// arm) each time a workspace's host stream is subscribed, and a daemon that
// has just taken a workspace over learns its page from that restatement.
//
// A CHANGE IS COUNTED ONCE; A RESTATEMENT IS NEVER COUNTED. The `loaded` and
// `unloaded` arms report a load or an unload that just happened and are each
// counted; `held` and `not_held` state what stands, change no counter, and
// set the state alone.
//
// FIRE AND FORGET. The report changes no behavior: a call that fails is
// recorded by Emacs and leaves the daemon's view stale only until the next
// report or the next restatement.

